  <!-- SECTION 2: TAXONOMIC & CLADE DIVERSITY -->
  <section id="panand-clades" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="panand-clades-title">
    <h2 id="panand-clades-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">Taxonomic &amp; Clade Diversity</h2>
    <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">The Pan-Andropogoneae dataset spans 15+ million years of panicoid grass evolution, structured across key taxonomic clades.</p>

    <div class="panand-clades-grid">
      <!-- Clade 1: Wild Zea / Teosintes -->
      <article class="panand-clade-card">
        <div class="panand-clade-head">
          <span class="panand-badge panand-badge-teosinte">Wild Zea / Teosintes</span>
          <span class="panand-clade-count">8 assemblies</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="panand-clade-name">Wild <em>Zea</em> &amp; Teosinte Lineages</h3>
        <p class="panand-clade-desc">Wild teosinte relatives including annual and perennial taxa. Features <em>Z. mays ssp. parviglumis</em> \(direct progenitor of domesticated maize\), <em>Z. mays ssp. mexicana</em> \(highland adapted\), <em>Z. diploperennis</em> \(perennial\), <em>Z. luxurians</em>, <em>Z. nicaraguensis</em> \(flood-tolerant\), and <em>Z. mays ssp. huehuetenangensis</em>.</p>
        <div class="panand-clade-members">
          <strong>Key Species&#58;</strong> <span><em>Z. parviglumis</em>, <em>Z. mexicana</em>, <em>Z. diploperennis</em>, <em>Z. nicaraguensis</em>, <em>Z. luxurians</em></span>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- Clade 2: Tripsacum -->
      <article class="panand-clade-card">
        <div class="panand-clade-head">
          <span class="panand-badge panand-badge-tripsacum">Tripsacum Clade</span>
          <span class="panand-clade-count">6 assemblies</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="panand-clade-name"><em>Tripsacum</em> Gamagrasses</h3>
        <p class="panand-clade-desc">The sister genus to <em>Zea</em>, native to the Americas. Includes northern and southern ecotypes of Eastern gamagrass \(<em>Tripsacum dactyloides</em>\) and <em>Tripsacum zopilotense</em>, displaying perennial growth habits, cold hardiness, and root architecture adaptations.</p>
        <div class="panand-clade-members">
          <strong>Key Species&#58;</strong> <span><em>T. dactyloides</em> \(FL south &amp; KS north\), <em>T. zopilotense</em></span>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- Clade 3: Andropogoneae Grasslands -->
      <article class="panand-clade-card">
        <div class="panand-clade-head">
          <span class="panand-badge panand-badge-grass">Bluestems &amp; Grasses</span>
          <span class="panand-clade-count">18 assemblies</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="panand-clade-name">Andropogoneae Dominant Grasslands</h3>
        <p class="panand-clade-desc">Ecologically dominant C4 prairie and savanna grasses representing millions of years of adaptation to heat, drought, fire, and grazing. Includes Big bluestem \(<em>A. gerardii</em>\), Little bluestem \(<em>S. scoparium</em>\), Yellow Indiangrass \(<em>S. nutans</em>\), and Silver bluestem \(<em>B. laguroides</em>\).</p>
        <div class="panand-clade-members">
          <strong>Key Species&#58;</strong> <span><em>Andropogon</em>, <em>Schizachyrium</em>, <em>Sorghastrum</em>, <em>Bothriochloa</em>, <em>Heteropogon</em></span>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- Clade 4: Aromatic & Specialty Taxa -->
      <article class="panand-clade-card">
        <div class="panand-clade-head">
          <span class="panand-badge panand-badge-outgroup">Specialty &amp; Wetland</span>
          <span class="panand-clade-count">4 assemblies</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="panand-clade-name">Aromatic, Wetland &amp; Specialty Grasses</h3>
        <p class="panand-clade-desc">Diverse panicoid grasses carrying specialized metabolic pathways and habitat adaptations, such as essential oil producing Lemongrass \(<em>C. citratus</em>\), Barbed wire grass \(<em>C. refractus</em>\), ornamental Baby bamboo \(<em>P. paniceum</em>\), and aquatic Hippo grass \(<em>V. cuspidata</em>\).</p>
        <div class="panand-clade-members">
          <strong>Key Species&#58;</strong> <span><em>Cymbopogon</em>, <em>Pogonatherum</em>, <em>Vossia</em>, <em>Elionorus</em></span>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3: KEY PUBLICATIONS & PREPRINTS -->
  <section id="panand-publications" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="panand-pub-title">
    <h2 id="panand-pub-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">Key Publications &amp; Preprints</h2>
    <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">Scientific literature detailing the Pan-Andropogoneae assemblies, polyploidy dynamics, and evolutionary constraint analyses.</p>

    <div class="panand-publications-grid">
      <!-- Publication 1: Stitzer et al. 2025 \(bioRxiv\) -->
      <article class="panand-pub-card">
        <div class="panand-pub-meta-top">
          <span class="panand-pub-journal-badge">bioRxiv &bull; 2025</span>
          <span class="panand-pub-doi">DOI&#58; 10.1101/2025.01.22.633974</span>
          <span class="panand-pub-award">NSF Award #1822330</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="panand-pub-title">Extensive genome evolution distinguishes maize within a stable tribe of grasses</h3>
        <p class="panand-pub-authors">Michelle C. Stitzer, Arun S. Seetharam, Armin Scheben, San-Kuo Hsu, Andrew J. Schulz, Tiffany AuBuchon-Elder, Matthew El-Walid, Tyler H. Ferebee, Christian O. Hale, Thien La, Zhi-Ying Liu, Samuel J. McMorrow, Philip Minx, Austin Phillips, Miranda Syring, Travis Wrightsman, Jiarui Zhai, Remy Pasquet, Colin McAllister, Simon Malcomber, Paweena Traiperm, Delanie Layton, Jane Zhong, Denise E. Costich, R. Kelly Dawe, Kevin Fengler, Carly Harris, Zachary Irelan, Victor Llaca, Preethi Parakkal, Gina Zastrow-Hayes, Margaret R. Woodhouse, Ethalinda K. Cannon, John L. Portwood II, Carson M. Andorf, Paul S. Albert, James A. Birchler, Adam Siepel, Jeffrey Ross-Ibarra, M. Cinta Romay, Elizabeth Kellogg, Edward S. Buckler, Matthew B. Hufford</p>
        <p class="panand-pub-citation"><em>bioRxiv</em> 2025.01.22.633974; doi&#58; https&#58;//doi.org/10.1101/2025.01.22.633974</p>
        
        <div class="panand-pub-abstract-wrap">
          <h4 class="panand-pub-abstract-heading">Abstract</h4>
          <p class="panand-pub-abstract-text">Over the last 20 million years, the Andropogoneae tribe of grasses has evolved to dominate 17% of global land area. Domestication of these grasses in the last 10,000 years has yielded our most productive crops, including maize, sugarcane, and sorghum. The majority of Andropogoneae species, including maize, show a history of polyploidy &mdash; a condition that, while offering the evolutionary advantage of multiple gene copies, poses challenges to basic cellular processes, gene expression, and epigenetic regulation. Genomic studies of polyploidy have been limited by sparse sampling of taxa in groups with multiple polyploidy events. Here, we present 33 genome assemblies from 27 species, including chromosome-scale assemblies of maize relatives <em>Zea</em> and <em>Tripsacum</em>. In maize, the after-effects of polyploidy have been widely studied, showing reduced chromosome number, biased fractionation of duplicate genes, and transposable element \(TE\) expansions. While we observe these patterns within the genus <em>Zea</em>, 12 other polyploidy events deviate significantly. Those tetraploids and hexaploids retain elevated chromosome number, maintain nearly complete complements of duplicate genes, and have only stochastic TE amplifications. These genomes reveal variable outcomes of polyploidy, challenging simple predictions and providing a foundation for understanding its evolutionary implications in an ecologically and economically important clade.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="panand-pub-actions">
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-primary" href="https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.01.22.633974v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read on bioRxiv &#8599;</a>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="https://nsf.gov/awardsearch/showAward?AWD_ID=1822330&HistoricalAwards" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NSF Award 1822330 &#8599;</a>
          <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-quiet" href="https://panandropogoneae.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PanAnd Consortium &#8599;</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4: DATA HOSTED AT MAIZEGDB -->
  <section id="panand-data-hosted" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="panand-hosted-title">
    <h2 id="panand-hosted-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">PanAnd Datasets Hosted at MaizeGDB</h2>
    <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">Explore integrated genome browser tracks, sequence BLAST targets, comparative synteny visualizers, and bulk download archives.</p>

    <div class="panand-hosted-grid">
      <!-- 1: JBrowse 2 -->
      <article class="panand-hosted-card">
        <h3>Genome Browsers \(JBrowse 2\)</h3>
        <p>Interactive linear and synteny comparison browsers across all 36 PanAnd reference assemblies with gene models, RNA-seq, and repeat annotations.</p>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="/genomebrowser">Open Genome Browsers &#8599;</a>
      </article>

      <!-- 2: BLAST -->
      <article class="panand-hosted-card">
        <h3>Sequence BLAST Tool</h3>
        <p>Align nucleotide or peptide sequences directly against full PanAnd founder assemblies, predicted CDS, and annotated gene model targets.</p>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="$(blast_url)">Run BLAST Search &#8599;</a>
      </article>

      <!-- 3: Bulk Downloads -->
      <article class="panand-hosted-card">
        <h3>Bulk Downloads &amp; Globus</h3>
        <p>Retrieve whole-genome FASTA sequences, GFF3 gene annotations, canonical CDS/PEP files, and cross-reference ID tables via HTTP or Globus.</p>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="/download#downloads-panand">Browse PanAnd Downloads &#8599;</a>
      </article>

      <!-- 4: CViT & Synteny -->
      <article class="panand-hosted-card">
        <h3>Comparative Synteny &amp; CViT</h3>
        <p>Whole-genome karyotype maps and chromosomal synteny alignments across wild <em>Zea</em>, <em>Tripsacum</em>, and Andropogoneae grass genomes.</p>
        <a class="mgdb-button mgdb-button-secondary" href="/popcorn/search/sequence_search/home.php?a=BLAST_UI">Launch Comparative Tools &#8599;</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5: PHYLOGENETIC FRAMEWORK & TREE -->
  <section id="panand-phylogeny" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="panand-phylo-title">
    <h2 id="panand-phylo-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">Phylogenetic Framework &amp; Evolutionary History</h2>
    <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">The Andropogoneae tribe of grasses contains a thousand species that collectively represent over a billion years of evolutionary history.</p>

    <div class="panand-phylo-content-wrap">
      <div class="panand-tree-img-box">
        <img src="/images/andropogoneae_tree.jpg" alt="Phylogenetic tree of the Andropogoneae grass tribe showing evolutionary divergence and sampled species" class="panand-tree-img" />
        <p class="panand-tree-caption">Figure&#58; Phylogenetic tree of the Andropogoneae tribe illustrating divergence times, photosynthetic adaptations \(NADP-C4\), and genome sampling across panicoid lineages.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="panand-phylo-text-box">
        <h3>Harnessing Convergence &amp; Constraint</h3>
        <p>Maize, sorghum, sugarcane, and <em>Miscanthus</em> are among the most productive and water-efficient crops and biofuels in the world, owing to a shared physiology and genetic ancestry spanning the last 15 million years. Beyond these major crops, the Andropogoneae tribe encompasses 800+ wild species dominating global grasslands that are adapted to environmental extremes including flooding, drought, heat, and frost.</p>
        <p>This project uses modern genomics and machine learning to survey and analyze these wild relatives, quantifying the disruptive impact of mutations in ancestral genomic elements and determining whether adaptive solutions to environmental stresses are convergently shared across species.</p>

        <div class="panand-toronto-box">
          <strong>Toronto Agreement Notice&#58;</strong>
          <p>Early-release genome assemblies and annotations are provided to the scientific community in accordance with the Toronto Agreement guidelines. Users are requested to respect publication rights prior to primary publication by the project consortium.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 6: FAQS & GUIDELINES -->
  <section id="panand-faqs" class="mgdb-panel" aria-labelledby="panand-faqs-title">
    <h2 id="panand-faqs-title" class="mgdb-panel-title">Frequently Asked Questions &amp; Guidelines</h2>
    <p class="mgdb-panel-lead">Guidance on data access, annotation versions, project funding, and community contributions.</p>

    <div class="mgdb-card-grid panand-faqs-grid">
      <!-- FAQ 1: Project Scope -->
      <article class="mgdb-card">
        <span class="mgdb-pill mgdb-pill-info">Overview</span>
        <h3>What is the Pan-Andropogoneae Project?</h3>
        <p>The PanAnd project is an NSF-funded multi-institutional research initiative \(NSF Award #1822330\) focused on assembling high-quality reference genomes across the Andropogoneae grass tribe to uncover evolutionary mechanisms of stress resilience and improve predictive breeding for maize and sorghum.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- FAQ 2: Release Rounds -->
      <article class="mgdb-card">
        <span class="mgdb-pill mgdb-pill-warn">Release Note</span>
        <h3>Round 1 vs. Round 2 Releases</h3>
        <p>Round 1 \(November 2022\) delivered 13 reference and draft genomes focused heavily on wild <em>Zea</em> teosintes and <em>Tripsacum</em>. Round 2 \(January 2025\) expanded the collection to 36 total assemblies, adding 23 new draft and reference genomes for diverse Andropogoneae prairie, savanna, and aromatic grasses.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- FAQ 3: Annotation Xref -->
      <article class="mgdb-card">
        <span class="mgdb-pill mgdb-pill-ok">Annotation</span>
        <h3>Gene Identifier Cross-References</h3>
        <p>Several Round 1 assemblies were initially released with preliminary gene annotations \(e.g., <code>aa.1</code>\). Official annotations \(e.g., <code>ab.1</code>\) provide enhanced gene models and sequential numbering. Downloadable cross-reference translation files are linked in the table above.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- FAQ 4: Feedback -->
      <article class="mgdb-card">
        <span class="mgdb-pill">Curation</span>
        <h3>Report Assembly or Gene Model Issues</h3>
        <p>If you identify sequence anomalies, assembly gaps, or problematic gene annotations in any PanAnd genome, please submit a report to MaizeGDB curators via the <a href="/curation/GenomeIssue/edit">Genome Issue Curation Form &#8599;</a> to assist ongoing community curation.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

